                        # Wait, let's re-check the derivation.
                        # sum_{i in I} R_i - sum_{j in J} C_j = (a-b)k - (c-d)k = (a-b-c+d)k
                        # sum_{i in I} sum_{j \notin J} x_{i,j} <= |I|(n-|J|) = (a+b)(n-c-d)
                        # Let's re-derive this.
                        # Sum_{i in I} R_i = Sum_{i in I} Sum_{j in J} x_{i,j} + Sum_{i in I} Sum_{j \notin J} x_{i,j}
                        # Sum_{i in I} R_i <= |I| |J| U + |I| (n - |J|) U
                        # This is just Sum_{i in I} R_i <= |I| n U.
                        # The condition for existence is Sum_{i in I} R_i - Sum_{j in J} C_j <= |I| (n - |J|) U.
                        # Wait, if I and J are disjoint, this is the one.
                        # What if they are not disjoint?
                        # Sum_{i in I} R_i = Sum_{i in I, j in J} x_{i,j} + Sum_{i in I, j \notin J} x_{i,j}
                        # Sum_{j in J} C_j = Sum_{j in J, i in I} x_{i,j} + Sum_{j in J, i \notin I} x_{i,j}
                        # Sum_{i in I} R_i - Sum_{j in J} C_j = Sum_{i in I, j \notin J} x_{i,j} - Sum_{j in J, i \notin I} x_{i,j}
                        # Since x_{i,j} \in [-1, 1], the maximum value of this is |I|(n-|J|) * 1 - 0.
                        # So Sum_{i in I} R_i - Sum_{j in J} C_j <= |I|(n-|J|).
                        # This is the correct condition.
                        min_val = min(min_val, numerator / denominator)
    return min_val

